# Shell scripts are checked out on Windows machines with core.autocrlf=true
# and get silently rewritten to CRLF, which then get COPY'd verbatim into
# Linux Docker images as docker-entrypoint.sh. The container's shell chokes
# on the trailing \r (e.g. `set -e` becomes `set -e\r`, producing
# "illegal option --" or "bad interpreter" and a crash-loop) — found the hard
# way across every plane's entrypoint scripts. Force LF regardless of local
# autocrlf settings so this cannot recur on a fresh Windows checkout.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Makefiles have the same problem with a sharper edge: a recipe line ending in
# a backslash continuation needs the backslash IMMEDIATELY before the newline.
# With CRLF the CR sits in between, so the continuation stops joining lines and
# the recipe runs as fragments (or dies on 'missing separator'). These are all
# stored LF in the blob already; this pins the CHECKOUT so a Windows clone with
# autocrlf=true cannot rewrite them.
Makefile text eol=lf
makefile text eol=lf
GNUmakefile text eol=lf
*.mk text eol=lf

# Source files: prettier defaults to endOfLine=lf, so a Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true makes eslint-plugin-prettier flag every CRLF line as
# "Delete `␍`" (~35k phantom errors in auth-core alone). All blobs are already
# stored LF (i/lf), so pinning eol=lf here only affects the working-tree
# checkout and produces no blob changes.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Deployment/contract files are read by Jest deployment-contract specs whose
# assertions match on '\n'; a CRLF checkout makes those tests fail on Windows.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Product-recording fixtures are hashed byte for byte: manifest.json lists a
# sourceHashes entry per file and tests/e2e/product-recording*.ts refuse to run
# when one differs. The blobs are stored LF, but a Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true rewrites the working copies to CRLF, which changes every
# hash and fails every take (seen 2026-09-29, after a merge). Pin the four
# scenario folders to LF so a checkout cannot change what the model is sent.
**/product-recordings/0[1-4]-*/* text eol=lf

# Contract goldens that are hashed byte for byte: SHA256SUMS pins every file of the directory,
# README.md included, and the producer vendors the directory unchanged. The .json goldens are
# already LF by the *.json rule; this pins the rest, so that a Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true cannot change a hash (the CR-T11 keyword-index parity contract).
**/testdata/index-parity-v1/* text eol=lf
